summary:
ECONOMYNEXT – Sri Lanka’s Ministry of Finance, Planning and Economic Development has called for long-term revenue increasing proposals to be submitted before June 12.

The ministry’s Revenue Management Committee (RMC) invited proposals, policy recommendations, and analytical submissions from think tanks, professional bodies, academic institutions, the private sector, non-governmental organizations registered in Sri Lanka, and any interested citizen to contribute to Sri Lanka’s medium to long-term revenue enhancement strategy.

“The RMC requests all parties to submit evidence-based proposals aligned with the Government’s medium to long-term objective of improving government revenue performance toward reaching 20 percent of GDP, through improved efficiency, broadened tax base, and formalization of the informal economy.”

Proposals may address, but are not limited to, the following areas:

– Medium- to long-term strategies to increase government revenue;
– Formalization of the informal economy and broadening of the tax base;
– Digitalization of revenue-collecting agencies to improve efficiency and transparency;
– Policy-consistent revenue mobilization measures;
– Measures to strengthen voluntary compliance and tax administration;
– Use of data analytics and technology-driven revenue systems; and
– International best practices in revenue administration and tax policy.

Registered think tanks, universities and research institutions, professional associations, private-sector entities, NGOs registered in Sri Lanka, and citizens with demonstrated expertise in fiscal policy can submit proposals.

Follow Now

They must include:
– A summary of the proposed revenue strategy, reform, or policy measure;
– The mechanism of executing the proposed strategy,
– Expected fiscal and/or administrative impact;
– Risks to implementing the proposed revenue strategy; and
– If an institution, the registered name and the number issued by the respective Sri Lankan authority, and, if a Sri Lankan citizen, the National Identity Card number, are required to be submitted voluntarily.

Proposals should be submitted in PDF format by email to dafp@fpd.treasury.gov.lk (Colombo/Jun1/2026)

